window.BOOK_DATA={"schemaVersion":2,"id":"ancient-japan-chapter-01","title":"Chapter 1 — The Earliest Societies in the Japanese Archipelago","language":"en","catalogs":{"maps":[{"type":"interactive","style":"../maps/atlas/ancient-japan-style.json","archive":"ancient-japan-vector.pmtiles","localArchive":"../maps/atlas/ancient-japan-vector.pmtiles","terrainTiles":"terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp","localTerrainTiles":"../maps/atlas/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp","plate":"1a","period":"jomon","sitePeriods":["jomon","yayoi"],"date":-300,"accent":"#80634E","showProvinces":false,"legend":[{"symbol":"terrain","label":"Relief and water","detail":"Terrain and water orient the view; neither marks a historical boundary."},{"symbol":"site","label":"Selected sites and study places","detail":"Dots are chapter-selected archaeological, historical, or thematic anchors."},{"symbol":"region","label":"Approximate historical zone","detail":"Tint and dashed edge mark an interpretive extent, not a fixed frontier."},{"symbol":"route","label":"Route or connection","detail":"Dashed cinnabar lines mark schematic historical or chapter-specific connections."},{"symbol":"river","label":"Major river","detail":"Blue lines appear as the map is enlarged."}],"view":{"bounds":[[127.0,29.0],[143.5,43.5]],"minZoom":3,"maxZoom":9},"metadata":{"slug":"ch01a_earliest_societies_internal","plateLabel":"MAP 1A","note":"Sites are selected study anchors; prehistoric shorelines and regional chronologies varied."},"id":"map-1a","src":"maps/Map_1a.webp","fallbackSrc":"maps/Map_1a.png","standing":true},{"type":"interactive","style":"../maps/atlas/ancient-japan-style.json","archive":"ancient-japan-vector.pmtiles","localArchive":"../maps/atlas/ancient-japan-vector.pmtiles","terrainTiles":"terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp","localTerrainTiles":"../maps/atlas/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp","plate":"1b","period":"yayoi","sitePeriods":["yayoi"],"date":150,"accent":"#B69149","showProvinces":false,"legend":[{"symbol":"terrain","label":"Relief and water","detail":"Terrain and water orient the view; neither marks a historical boundary."},{"symbol":"site","label":"Selected sites and study places","detail":"Dots are chapter-selected archaeological, historical, or thematic anchors."},{"symbol":"region","label":"Approximate historical zone","detail":"Tint and dashed edge mark an interpretive extent, not a fixed frontier."},{"symbol":"route","label":"Route or connection","detail":"Dashed cinnabar lines mark schematic historical or chapter-specific connections."},{"symbol":"river","label":"Major river","detail":"Blue lines appear as the map is enlarged."}],"view":{"bounds":[[120.5,27.0],[142.0,44.0]],"minZoom":3,"maxZoom":9},"metadata":{"slug":"ch01b_earliest_societies_east_asia","plateLabel":"MAP 1B","note":"Wa is retained as an external collective label, not a modern national boundary."},"id":"map-1b","src":"maps/Map_1b.webp","fallbackSrc":"maps/Map_1b.png","standing":true}],"timeline":[{"id":"event-981cc44422","type":"point","label":"Kojiki completed","display_date":"AD 712","certainty":"secure","caption":"A court compilation of myth, genealogy, and remembered tradition.","start":712,"end":712},{"id":"event-d1809f0d88","type":"point","label":"Nihon Shoki completed","display_date":"AD 720","certainty":"secure","caption":"The court's chronicle framed the prehistoric past from an eighth-century political world.","start":720,"end":720},{"id":"event-223ce36a66","type":"span","label":"Earliest secure occupation","start":-38000,"end":-35000,"display_date":"ca. 38,000–35,000 years ago","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Secure evidence appears in the main islands and Ryukyu chain during this broad interval."},{"id":"event-0674f578e3","type":"point","label":"Paleolithic hoax exposed","display_date":"AD 2000","certainty":"secure","caption":"Exposure of planted artifacts forced reassessment of claims for much earlier occupation.","start":2000,"end":2000},{"id":"event-d3daf4ab91","type":"point","label":"Aira eruption","display_date":"ca. 30,000 years ago","certainty":"approximate","caption":"The eruption left a widespread ash marker and transformed southern environments.","start":-28000,"end":-28000},{"id":"event-e064649c20","type":"span","label":"Late Paleolithic","start":-38000,"end":-14500,"display_date":"ca. 38,000–14,500 years ago","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Regional stone industries and mobile lifeways developed across the archipelago."},{"id":"event-074c1cbc1c","type":"point","label":"Odai Yamamoto I pottery","display_date":"ca. 16,500 years ago","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Calibrated residue dates place pottery production in the closing Pleistocene.","start":-14500,"end":-14500},{"id":"event-cf42a83706","type":"span","label":"Incipient Jomon","start":-14500,"end":-10000,"display_date":"ca. 14,500–10,000 BC","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Pottery and new subsistence practices spread unevenly before the Holocene."},{"id":"event-26d5cd2101","type":"span","label":"Jomon sequence","start":-14500,"end":-900,"display_date":"ca. 14,500 BC–regional first millennium BC endings","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Dates and period boundaries differ by region."},{"id":"event-3818a0753a","type":"span","label":"Sannai Maruyama occupation","start":-3900,"end":-2200,"display_date":"ca. 3900–2200 BC","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Repeated construction formed a durable settlement and gathering place in northern Honshu."},{"id":"event-7a7c7469ba","type":"span","label":"Early to Middle Jomon growth","start":-5000,"end":-2500,"display_date":"ca. 5000–2500 BC","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Many regions show denser settlement and intensified woodland and aquatic resource use."},{"id":"event-b2a35c32bc","type":"span","label":"Long-distance Jomon exchange","start":-10000,"end":-900,"display_date":"throughout the Jomon sequence","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Obsidian, jade, asphalt, amber, and shell moved through overlapping regional networks."},{"id":"event-7b56ea546a","type":"span","label":"Dogu traditions","start":-10000,"end":-300,"display_date":"ca. 10,000–300 BC, regionally","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Clay figurines varied substantially across time and place."},{"id":"event-94a3e55f86","type":"span","label":"Late and Final Jomon reorganization","start":-2500,"end":-900,"display_date":"ca. 2500–900 BC, regionally","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Settlement contraction and new ritual and exchange patterns unfolded unevenly."},{"id":"event-0b2cb4314e","type":"point","label":"Wet-rice farming in northern Kyushu","display_date":"by the tenth century BC","certainty":"approximate","caption":"AMS dating places early paddy agriculture centuries earlier than the older conventional chronology.","start":-950,"end":-950},{"id":"event-e8c2c9f9ee","type":"span","label":"Agricultural dispersal","start":-950,"end":-200,"display_date":"ca. tenth–second centuries BC","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Farming spread east at different rates while mixed economies continued."},{"id":"event-e5e47246a2","type":"span","label":"Yayoi period","start":-950,"end":250,"display_date":"ca. tenth century BC–third century AD, regionally","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Agricultural villages, hierarchy, exchange, and conflict developed unevenly."},{"id":"event-3a0b0c4ffe","type":"span","label":"Yoshinogari growth","start":-400,"end":250,"display_date":"ca. fourth century BC–third century AD","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Successive phases created a major enclosed center in northern Kyushu."},{"id":"event-ecd1b94bb2","type":"span","label":"Bronze ritual traditions","start":-400,"end":200,"display_date":"ca. fourth century BC–second century AD","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Weapon-shaped bronzes, dotaku bells, and mirrors marked changing ritual and political networks."},{"id":"event-8a5ec331b6","type":"span","label":"Yayoi migration and admixture","start":-950,"end":300,"display_date":"from the first millennium BC","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Population movement and mixture continued at different rates and extended into the Kofun age."},{"id":"event-c46ec45ddf","type":"point","label":"Na embassy to Later Han","display_date":"AD 57","certainty":"secure","caption":"The Later Han history records an embassy and an imperial seal.","start":57,"end":57},{"id":"event-007b5c0ea5","type":"point","label":"Wa embassy","display_date":"AD 107","certainty":"secure","caption":"A second recorded embassy reached the Later Han court.","start":107,"end":107},{"id":"event-49c2a9678d","type":"span","label":"Wei account of Wa","start":220,"end":265,"display_date":"third century AD","certainty":"secure","caption":"The dynastic history describes many Wa polities and the federation of Yamatai."},{"id":"event-855fc98b77","type":"point","label":"Himiko's embassy to Wei","display_date":"AD 238","certainty":"secure","caption":"Yamatai entered a formal diplomatic relationship with the Wei court.","start":238,"end":238},{"id":"event-f3e1071557","type":"point","label":"Death of Himiko","display_date":"ca. AD 247–248","certainty":"approximate","caption":"The account reports burial, disorder, and a later female succession.","start":248,"end":248},{"id":"event-510a3bdcb6","type":"span","label":"Earliest keyhole-mound horizon","start":250,"end":300,"display_date":"later third century AD","certainty":"approximate","caption":"Large standardized mounds signal the political transformation developed in the next chapter."}],"illustrations":[]},"sections":[{"id":"01.01","number":"01.01","title":"Before Japan","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.01-p001","text":"The story begins before there was a country called Japan.\nThe long island chain held many communities, languages, and ways of life, but no court commanded them all and no border enclosed a Japanese people.\nIt is therefore safer to speak of the Japanese archipelago, and later of the communities that outsiders called Wa.\nOnly near the end of this chapter do we approach a political world from which the Yamato polity would emerge.\nEven then, unity was an ambition rather than an accomplished fact.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-981cc44422","event-d1809f0d88"],"genealogy":[{"id":"AJ1-mythic-line","caption":"This is a retrospective court genealogy, not a contemporary record of prehistoric succession.","active":["amaterasu","ninigi","jimmu"]}]},{"id":"01.01-p002","text":"The oldest Japanese books tell a different kind of beginning.\nThe Kojiki, compiled in 712, and the Nihon Shoki, completed in 720, lead the imperial line from the sun deity Amaterasu to Jimmu, the first human sovereign in their genealogy.\nThey place Jimmu's accession in 660 BC and describe his eastward advance to the Yamato basin.\nThese works preserve court memory, ritual claims, poetry, and political theology from a much later age.\nThey are indispensable evidence for the eighth-century court, but they are not contemporary reports of the distant prehistoric past.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-981cc44422","event-d1809f0d88"],"genealogy":[{"id":"AJ1-mythic-line","caption":"This is a retrospective court genealogy, not a contemporary record of prehistoric succession.","active":["amaterasu","ninigi","jimmu"]}]},{"id":"01.01-p003","text":"Archaeology supplies a different archive.\nStone flakes, hearths, storage pits, shell mounds, plant remains, human bones, fields, weapons, and imported objects survive without explaining themselves.\nTheir dates and contexts can be tested, yet every interpretation remains an argument made from incomplete traces.\nThe written record begins from outside the archipelago, in Chinese dynastic histories concerned with embassies, tribute, geography, and political marvels.\nThese observers saw coastal envoys and reported polities, not an entire population.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-981cc44422","event-d1809f0d88"],"genealogy":[{"id":"AJ1-mythic-line","caption":"This is a retrospective court genealogy, not a contemporary record of prehistoric succession.","active":["amaterasu","ninigi","jimmu"]}]},{"id":"01.01-p004","text":"This chapter keeps those archives distinct.\nIt uses material remains to reconstruct Paleolithic, Jomon, and Yayoi lives; Chinese testimony to glimpse the later communities of Wa; and Japanese chronicles to study retrospective ancestry.\nModern genetic evidence adds another history, one of population movement and mixture.\nNo single archive cancels the others, and none should be turned into a simple origin story for a modern nation.\nTogether they reveal an archipelago repeatedly made by movement across water.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-981cc44422","event-d1809f0d88"],"genealogy":[{"id":"AJ1-mythic-line","caption":"This is a retrospective court genealogy, not a contemporary record of prehistoric succession.","active":["amaterasu","ninigi","jimmu"]}]}]},{"id":"01.02","number":"01.02","title":"The First Crossings","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.02-p001","text":"The earliest secure archaeological traces of modern humans in the main islands date to roughly thirty-eight thousand years ago.\nClaims for a much earlier occupation once attracted great attention, but the Paleolithic hoax exposed in 2000 forced a severe reexamination of sites and methods.\nEvidence earlier than about forty thousand years remains unconfirmed under the strongest standards of workmanship, stratigraphy, and repeated association.\nThat caution is not an absence of history.\nIt is a reminder that a trustworthy beginning must be earned layer by layer.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-223ce36a66","event-0674f578e3"]},{"id":"01.02-p002","text":"Lower sea levels changed the outline of East Asia during the last ice age, yet they did not turn every channel into dry land.\nHokkaido was connected northward through Sakhalin at times, while Kyushu lay closer to the Korean Peninsula.\nThe main islands formed a larger landmass, but deep or persistent straits still interrupted the routes from the south and toward offshore islands.\nPeople entered by more than one path and adapted to sharply different environments.\nThe archipelago was a meeting ground, not the terminus of one marching column.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-223ce36a66","event-0674f578e3"]},{"id":"01.02-p003","text":"Obsidian makes some of these journeys visible.\nStone from Kozushima, an island south of central Honshu, appears at mainland sites from the opening centuries of the Late Paleolithic.\nObtaining it required purposeful sea travel and a return crossing over water beyond the horizon.\nFarther south, human remains and sites in the Ryukyu chain show that voyagers reached islands that had never been joined to the continent by a simple land bridge.\nBoats have vanished, but the distribution of stone and people preserves the fact of navigation.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-223ce36a66","event-0674f578e3"]},{"id":"01.02-p004","text":"The first settlers did not enter an empty stage with a fixed script.\nThey learned volcanic landscapes, river terraces, coasts, forests, and cold grasslands whose plants and animals shifted with climate.\nTheir descendants were not isolated from the continent forever, nor can every later inhabitant be traced to a single founding band.\nMovement continued in both directions across the northern, western, and southern seas.\nThe earliest history of the islands is thus already an East Asian and maritime history.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-223ce36a66","event-0674f578e3"]}]},{"id":"01.03","number":"01.03","title":"Life in the Late Ice Age","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.03-p001","text":"Paleolithic communities moved through territories larger than any surviving camp.\nSmall groups returned to favorable terraces, river crossings, stone sources, and hunting grounds, leaving dense scatters in some places and only a few flakes in others.\nTheir tools included backed blades, trapezoids, points, scrapers, and later microblades set into composite implements.\nTool traditions varied by region and changed through time.\nThey were learned techniques, not an evolutionary ladder from crude to refined.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-d3daf4ab91","event-e064649c20"]},{"id":"01.03-p002","text":"The great eruption of the Aira caldera in southern Kyushu, around thirty thousand years ago, spread ash across much of the archipelago.\nIt transformed habitats and created a chronological marker that archaeologists can follow over enormous distances.\nPeople endured this unstable world by moving, regrouping, and altering what they made.\nCold phases favored large game and open-country resources in some regions, while coasts and forests offered different possibilities elsewhere.\nNo single Paleolithic diet can represent the whole chain of islands.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-d3daf4ab91","event-e064649c20"]},{"id":"01.03-p003","text":"Hunting mattered, but the surviving stone points can distort our view.\nWooden tools, baskets, cordage, hides, gathered plants, and most prepared food rarely survive in acidic soil.\nWomen, men, children, and elders maintained camps and knowledge through labor that left little stone behind.\nSeasonal judgment was as important as a sharp edge.\nA community had to remember when nuts ripened, where animals passed, which shore was safe, and how distant groups could be reached.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-d3daf4ab91","event-e064649c20"]},{"id":"01.03-p004","text":"By the final millennia of the ice age, microblade technologies linked the islands to broad northern and eastern Asian traditions.\nOther tool forms and routes point toward the Korean Peninsula and the Pacific rim.\nThese resemblances do not prove that whole peoples moved whenever a tool changed.\nObjects can travel through exchange, imitation, marriage, or the relocation of a few skilled makers.\nThe archaeological record shows contact and choice more securely than it shows named populations.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-d3daf4ab91","event-e064649c20"]}]},{"id":"01.04","number":"01.04","title":"Pottery at the Edge of the Pleistocene","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.04-p001","text":"At Odai Yamamoto I in northern Honshu, charred residue on pottery has been dated to roughly sixteen thousand five hundred years ago.\nOther early vessels across East Asia show that pottery was not simply invented after farming or permanent village life.\nIt appeared among mobile or partly settled foragers near the end of the last ice age.\nThe old equation of pottery with an agricultural revolution does not fit this evidence.\nA container could change cooking long before a field changed the landscape.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-074c1cbc1c","event-cf42a83706"]},{"id":"01.04-p002","text":"The first pots were scarce, and their use must not be imagined from later abundance.\nThey could simmer fish, meat, nuts, and plants; render oils; soften foods; and hold meals that joined a group around a hearth.\nResidues from early ceramics often point to aquatic resources, though uses differed by place and vessel.\nPottery was heavy and breakable, so even occasional production suggests repeated returns or shorter seasonal rounds.\nIt marks a change in practice, not an instant conversion to settled life.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-074c1cbc1c","event-cf42a83706"]},{"id":"01.04-p003","text":"Archaeologists call this opening ceramic phase Incipient Jomon.\nThe term Jomon means cord marking, but the earliest vessels display several techniques, and not every later Jomon pot carries the same decoration.\nPeriod names are modern tools for ordering evidence.\nThey should not be mistaken for the names by which ancient people knew themselves.\nNor did one cultural boundary fall across Hokkaido, Honshu, Shikoku, Kyushu, and the Ryukyus at the same moment.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-074c1cbc1c","event-cf42a83706"]},{"id":"01.04-p004","text":"As the climate warmed after the last glacial maximum, forests expanded and coastlines moved inland.\nNew plants, fish runs, shellfish beds, and woodland animals supported more concentrated use of favored places.\nThe bow became prominent, pit dwellings more visible, and ceramic traditions more varied.\nThese changes unfolded over millennia and at different tempos.\nThe Jomon world emerged through accumulated experiments in mobility, food, storage, social ties, and ritual.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-074c1cbc1c","event-cf42a83706"]}]},{"id":"01.05","number":"01.05","title":"Many Jomon Worlds","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.05-p001","text":"The Jomon period endured for more than ten thousand years in much of the main archipelago.\nIts conventional stages, from Incipient and Initial through Early, Middle, Late, and Final, organize changes in pottery and settlement.\nAbsolute dates vary by region and by the calibration of radiocarbon samples.\nNorthern Kyushu entered an agricultural Yayoi sequence centuries before some eastern and northern communities did.\nA single national timetable therefore conceals the very diversity it is meant to explain.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-26d5cd2101"]},{"id":"01.05-p002","text":"Environmental zones encouraged different economies.\nThe rich deciduous forests of northeastern Honshu offered chestnuts, walnuts, acorns, game, and salmon.\nThe Pacific and Inland Sea coasts supplied shellfish and many kinds of fish, while southwestern communities drew on warmer forests and maritime routes.\nHokkaido and the Ryukyus followed histories connected to, but never identical with, those of central Japan.\nJomon was a family of regional lifeways joined by overlapping practices rather than one uniform tribe.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-26d5cd2101"]},{"id":"01.05-p003","text":"Pottery makes regional identity unusually visible.\nCommunities shaped clay with local tempers, built forms suited to cooking and serving, and marked surfaces with cords, shells, incisions, and modeled ornament.\nStyles spread, met, and changed at their borders.\nA vessel could be useful equipment, a display of skill, and a carrier of social memory at once.\nIts decoration mapped networks of learning that cannot be reduced to political frontiers.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-26d5cd2101"]},{"id":"01.05-p004","text":"Population also rose and fell unevenly.\nSite numbers suggest substantial growth in parts of central and northeastern Honshu during the Early and Middle Jomon, followed by contraction and reorganization.\nSuch counts do not translate neatly into a census, because one group could leave many sites and preservation differs by landscape.\nEven so, they show that the density of life shifted dramatically over time.\nThe long Jomon sequence was dynamic, not a changeless age before history.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-26d5cd2101"]}]},{"id":"01.06","number":"01.06","title":"Houses, Settlements, and Memory","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.06-p001","text":"Pit dwellings were built by setting a floor below ground level and raising a roof on posts.\nTheir shapes, hearths, and rebuilding histories varied, but many formed clusters around open spaces.\nAt some sites, storage pits, graves, refuse zones, and paths organized a settlement beyond the household.\nA village was not merely a row of shelters.\nIt was a remembered arrangement of work, ancestry, hospitality, and return.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-3818a0753a"]},{"id":"01.06-p002","text":"Sannai Maruyama in northern Honshu reveals the scale that Jomon settlement could reach during portions of the Early and Middle periods.\nHundreds of building traces accumulated there over many generations, with long houses, storage pits, graves, and large post settings.\nThe site was not necessarily a dense town occupied at the same size throughout its history.\nIts importance lies in repeated construction, far-reaching exchange, and durable attachment to place.\nMonumentality could grow without a palace, army, or writing.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-3818a0753a"]},{"id":"01.06-p003","text":"Settlement did not eliminate mobility.\nPeople could maintain a central village while visiting fishing stations, hunting grounds, nut groves, clay beds, and stone sources.\nSome houses were rebuilt in the same area; other sites were occupied briefly or seasonally.\nThe useful contrast is therefore not between wandering and settled people.\nIt is between different schedules of movement, each anchored by particular relationships and resources.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-3818a0753a"]},{"id":"01.06-p004","text":"Large structures required cooperation, but they do not by themselves prove a ruling class.\nHouse size, grave goods, diet, and access to valued materials sometimes reveal differences, yet most Jomon communities lack the sharp hierarchy of later chiefly societies.\nAuthority may have depended on age, ritual knowledge, skill, generosity, or the ability to connect households.\nLeadership could be real without becoming an office inherited through a state.\nThe evidence invites a spectrum of arrangements rather than a choice between equality and kingship.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-3818a0753a"]}]},{"id":"01.07","number":"01.07","title":"A Managed Abundance","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.07-p001","text":"Jomon subsistence is often called hunting and gathering, but the phrase can sound more passive than the evidence allows.\nPeople burned vegetation, tended useful trees, scheduled harvests, processed toxins, stored food, and reshaped habitats around settlements.\nChestnut stands near major sites may reflect deliberate encouragement and transplantation.\nThis was not field agriculture on the Yayoi model, yet it was skilled landscape management.\nThe border between wild and cultivated was a zone of practice.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-7a7c7469ba"]},{"id":"01.07-p002","text":"Nuts were abundant but laborious.\nAcorns often required leaching; chestnuts and walnuts had to be collected, dried, cracked, and stored against damp and animals.\nGrinding stones, pits, and charred remains reveal work distributed across seasons.\nPlant food could support a community only through planning and repeated knowledge.\nIts archaeological invisibility once made meat seem more dominant than it was.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-7a7c7469ba"]},{"id":"01.07-p003","text":"Coastal shell mounds preserve another unusually rich archive.\nThey contain shell, fish bone, mammal bone, tools, ornaments, hearth waste, and sometimes burials.\nTheir contents record changing shorelines as well as meals.\nIn the Kanto region, the warm-water Jomon transgression flooded low valleys and drew the sea deep into areas now inland.\nLater falling seas moved estuaries and forced communities to revise familiar routes.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-7a7c7469ba"]},{"id":"01.07-p004","text":"Fishing technologies included hooks, harpoons, nets, weirs, dugout canoes, and knowledge of tidal and seasonal movement.\nSome communities pursued offshore species; others concentrated on rivers, bays, or shell beds.\nDogs assisted hunting and sometimes received careful burial, suggesting relationships beyond simple utility.\nFood linked people to animals, plants, waters, and places through customary obligations we can only partly recover.\nAbundance was produced by attention, not granted automatically by nature.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-7a7c7469ba"]}]},{"id":"01.08","number":"01.08","title":"Exchange Without a State","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.08-p001","text":"Jomon exchange carried materials far beyond their sources.\nObsidian moved from islands and mountain deposits, jade from the Itoigawa region traveled widely, and asphalt, amber, shell ornaments, and finished objects crossed regional boundaries.\nNo royal road or merchant bureaucracy directed these movements.\nThey passed through visits, partnerships, marriage, seasonal gatherings, gifts, and chains of exchange.\nDistance reveals connection but not the exact social contract behind it.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-b2a35c32bc"]},{"id":"01.08-p002","text":"Some materials mattered because they were technically excellent.\nObsidian fractures into a sharp edge, while asphalt can haft a point and repair a vessel.\nOther objects drew value from color, rarity, origin, workmanship, or the histories carried with them.\nA jade pendant was more than transported stone once it had moved through hands and ceremonies.\nExchange joined practical need to reputation and memory.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-b2a35c32bc"]},{"id":"01.08-p003","text":"Craft specialization existed in degrees.\nFine lacquer, complex basketry impressions, shell bracelets, carved objects, and elaborate pottery demanded trained judgment and time.\nA skilled maker might work for a household, a seasonal gathering, or a wider network without belonging to a permanent occupational class.\nThe same object could circulate between economic and ritual settings.\nModern categories divide what ancient practice may have kept together.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-b2a35c32bc"]},{"id":"01.08-p004","text":"Networks also transmitted styles and information.\nPottery forms could move without clay vessels traveling, because visitors remembered techniques or potters relocated.\nMarriage linked communities biologically and politically long before written treaties.\nNews about harvests, conflict, dangerous routes, and ceremonies followed the same paths as valued goods.\nThe archipelago was socially connected at many scales, even though no center controlled the whole.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-b2a35c32bc"]}]},{"id":"01.09","number":"01.09","title":"Bodies, Ritual, and Belonging","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.09-p001","text":"Jomon graves usually occur within or near places of living.\nThe dead were flexed, extended, placed in pits or jars, sometimes sprinkled with pigment, and occasionally accompanied by ornaments or tools.\nTreatment varied by age, region, and period.\nBurial close to houses made ancestry part of the inhabited landscape.\nIt also gives archaeologists a biased sample, since acidic soils destroy bone across much of the archipelago.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-7b56ea546a"]},{"id":"01.09-p002","text":"Some people underwent intentional tooth removal or filing according to patterned rules.\nThese modifications were visible signs carried through life and may have marked age, marriage, affiliation, mourning, or ritual status.\nThe exact meanings remain debated.\nTheir disciplined repetition nevertheless shows that belonging could be inscribed on the body.\nIdentity was made through social acts, not merely inherited as biological descent.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-7b56ea546a"]},{"id":"01.09-p003","text":"Clay figurines called dogu appear in many forms, especially in eastern Japan.\nMany emphasize faces, breasts, bellies, or limbs, and many were broken before deposition.\nThey have been interpreted as images of fertility, healing, ancestors, spirits, or ritual substitution.\nNo one explanation fits every figure across thousands of years.\nWhat is secure is that modeling and breaking clay participated in consequential acts beyond ordinary cooking.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-7b56ea546a"]},{"id":"01.09-p004","text":"Stone rods, circles, arranged deposits, lacquered objects, and unusual buildings expand the ritual field.\nCeremony may have gathered dispersed communities and helped negotiate marriage, exchange, grief, and seasonal renewal.\nRitual was not a decorative layer placed over subsistence.\nIt organized relations among households and between humans and a more-than-human world.\nThose relations helped communities endure without becoming identical to later shrines, clans, or imperial rites.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-7b56ea546a"]}]},{"id":"01.10","number":"01.10","title":"Contraction and Regional Change","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.10-p001","text":"After the Middle Jomon peak, many areas of central and northeastern Honshu show fewer or smaller settlements.\nClimate became cooler and in some places less favorable to resources on which large communities had relied.\nCoastlines changed, forests shifted, and local overuse may also have mattered.\nNo single environmental cause explains every region.\nPeople responded through movement, new exchange ties, altered settlement, and different combinations of food.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-94a3e55f86"]},{"id":"01.10-p002","text":"Later Jomon communities often formed smaller networks while developing intricate ritual objects and cemeteries.\nIn some areas, collective burial and stone arrangements made affiliation visible across households.\nThe reduction of settlement size did not mean cultural impoverishment.\nIt could concentrate social investment in gatherings, mortuary practice, and portable objects.\nComplexity changed its form.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-94a3e55f86"]},{"id":"01.10-p003","text":"Contacts with the continent never ceased.\nIn northern Kyushu, Korean Peninsula crops and artifacts appeared within Final Jomon settings before irrigated rice became dominant.\nIn Hokkaido, northern exchange connected communities toward Sakhalin and the Amur region.\nThe Ryukyus maintained maritime worlds oriented through their own island chains.\nThe approaching Yayoi transition was therefore one of several regional transformations, not a wave that struck an undifferentiated Jomon shore.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-94a3e55f86"]},{"id":"01.10-p004","text":"The word transition can also mislead by making one period seem destined to become the next.\nCommunities adopted new plants, tools, and customs selectively.\nSome combined them with older practices for centuries, while others resisted or remained beyond the agricultural frontier.\nWhat later archaeologists group as Jomon and Yayoi overlapped in time and space.\nThe boundary is a useful pattern in evidence, not a line ancient people crossed in a single step.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-94a3e55f86"]}]},{"id":"01.11","number":"01.11","title":"Rice Comes Through the Straits","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.11-p001","text":"Irrigated rice cultivation began in northern Kyushu by about the tenth century BC, earlier than the chronology once used in many surveys.\nThe first secure paddies, field channels, tools, and associated pottery belong to a system already developed through East Asian farming histories.\nRice and millet had moved from China into the Korean Peninsula long before they crossed to Kyushu.\nTheir arrival in the archipelago joined a chain of knowledge, crops, and people.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-0b2cb4314e","event-e8c2c9f9ee"]},{"id":"01.11-p002","text":"The crossing was short in distance but large in consequence.\nMigrants from or through the southern Korean Peninsula brought farming techniques, new pottery practices, and forms of settlement and burial.\nLocal communities contributed environmental knowledge, labor, exchange partners, and established ways of living.\nThe resulting Yayoi societies were not copies of a continental original.\nThey were made in the archipelago through collaboration, competition, intermarriage, and adaptation.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-0b2cb4314e","event-e8c2c9f9ee"]},{"id":"01.11-p003","text":"A paddy was a social machine as much as an agricultural plot.\nPeople had to clear and level ground, control water, maintain banks, coordinate planting, and protect a harvest that ripened at one vulnerable time.\nWooden spades, hoes, rakes, and harvesting knives multiplied the work that survives in wet soils.\nStorage raised questions about contribution, distribution, and authority.\nRice created new possibilities for surplus, but no field produced a state by itself.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-0b2cb4314e","event-e8c2c9f9ee"]},{"id":"01.11-p004","text":"Agriculture spread eastward unevenly through western Honshu and Shikoku, then into central regions.\nCold climate, local ecology, and existing social choices slowed or redirected it in the northeast.\nForaging, fishing, and hunting continued within farming communities, while dry-field crops accompanied rice.\nThe Yayoi economy was never rice alone.\nIts novelty lay in the growing weight of cultivated harvests and the institutions built around land and water.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-0b2cb4314e","event-e8c2c9f9ee"]}]},{"id":"01.12","number":"01.12","title":"The Yayoi Village","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.12-p001","text":"Many Yayoi settlements combined pit dwellings with raised-floor storehouses and carefully divided work areas.\nSome stood beside paddies on low plains; others occupied defensible uplands.\nDitches enclosed a number of villages, especially during periods of tension.\nThe settlement became an arena where household labor met collective decisions about water, storage, defense, and ceremony.\nIts plan reveals new coordination without proving that every village had a king.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-e5e47246a2","event-3a0b0c4ffe"]},{"id":"01.12-p002","text":"At Yoshinogari in northern Kyushu, ditches, palisades, large buildings, storage, and burials accumulated in a major regional center.\nThe visible remains belong to several phases and should not be frozen into one simultaneous city.\nEven so, the site shows how large communities could organize protected spaces and distinguish important compounds.\nSimilar processes occurred at different scales elsewhere.\nRegional centers grew through networks of villages rather than appearing fully formed.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-e5e47246a2","event-3a0b0c4ffe"]},{"id":"01.12-p003","text":"Violence becomes unusually clear in parts of the Yayoi record.\nHuman bones bear projectile wounds and other trauma, settlements burn, and ditches separate inhabitants from attackers.\nCompetition may have concerned water, land, stored grain, routes, and political allegiance.\nYet weapons in graves or ritual deposits do not always record combat.\nA blade could display status or address supernatural power after it ceased to be practical equipment.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-e5e47246a2","event-3a0b0c4ffe"]},{"id":"01.12-p004","text":"Burial expressed increasing difference.\nNorthern Kyushu communities used jar coffins, wooden coffins, stone settings, and graves supplied in sharply unequal ways.\nSome individuals received bronze mirrors, weapons, ornaments, or other scarce goods, while neighboring burials remained plain.\nSuch contrasts suggest hereditary advantage and emerging rank in certain regions.\nThey do not imply one uniform class system across the Yayoi world.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-e5e47246a2","event-3a0b0c4ffe"]}]},{"id":"01.13","number":"01.13","title":"Metal, Prestige, and Sacred Power","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.13-p001","text":"Bronze and iron reached the archipelago through continental networks, but their histories diverged.\nIron edges improved woodworking and agriculture and eventually entered many practical tasks, though early supply was limited and much material came through the Korean Peninsula.\nBronze was often directed toward ceremony and display.\nIts value lay not only in strength but in controlled access to foreign materials, casting knowledge, and resonant form.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-ecd1b94bb2"]},{"id":"01.13-p002","text":"Bronze weapons enlarged beyond useful fighting proportions in parts of western Japan.\nDaggers, spears, and halberds became thin ceremonial objects, deposited in graves or caches.\nIn the Kinki region and farther east, dotaku bells developed from small functional models into large, richly decorated bronzes.\nMany were buried away from settlements without accompanying human remains.\nTheir sound, images, and concealment suggest rites tied to community and agricultural renewal.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-ecd1b94bb2"]},{"id":"01.13-p003","text":"The distributions of weapon-shaped bronzes and dotaku once encouraged maps of rival cultural blocs.\nThose patterns are real but shifting, and objects crossed their apparent boundaries.\nThey may mark networks of ritual practice, production, exchange, or alliance rather than fixed ethnic territories.\nLater standardization and wider circulation accompanied the growth of broader political relationships.\nMaterial style can reveal a political field without naming its rulers.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-ecd1b94bb2"]},{"id":"01.13-p004","text":"Chinese mirrors carried special authority.\nSome arrived as diplomatic gifts or trade goods; others were copied in the archipelago.\nTheir gleaming surfaces, foreign inscriptions, and association with elite graves made distant connections visible.\nControl over imported iron, bronze, beads, and prestige objects helped leaders convert external relationships into local standing.\nContinental diplomacy and village hierarchy were becoming parts of the same system.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-ecd1b94bb2"]}]},{"id":"01.14","number":"01.14","title":"Migration and Admixture","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.14-p001","text":"The Yayoi transformation involved the movement of people as well as ideas.\nSkeletal differences in northern Kyushu, archaeological links across the straits, and ancient DNA all support migration from continental East Asia through the Korean Peninsula.\nThe scale and timing varied by region.\nThere was no single invasion that replaced every Jomon community, and no purely local adoption that left population history unchanged.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-8a5ec331b6"]},{"id":"01.14-p002","text":"Genomes from ancient individuals reveal mixture rather than two sealed races.\nJomon-related ancestry was deeply divergent within East Asia, while later migrants contributed ancestry associated with Northeast and East Asian populations.\nRecent models often distinguish three major ancestral components in present-day mainland Japanese populations.\nThe third is especially visible in people of the Kofun age and after, reminding us that migration continued beyond the Yayoi transition.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-8a5ec331b6"]},{"id":"01.14-p003","text":"Ancient DNA also exposes regional coexistence.\nSome Yayoi-period people in northwestern Kyushu retained predominantly Jomon-related ancestry even after farming and migrant communities were established nearby.\nCulture and ancestry did not travel in perfect lockstep.\nA person could use Yayoi pottery or live within a farming landscape without possessing one imagined Yayoi biological identity.\nCommunities mixed at different rates and under different social conditions.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-8a5ec331b6"]},{"id":"01.14-p004","text":"Modern populations cannot be projected backward as unchanged descendants.\nAinu, Ryukyuan, and mainland Japanese people retain different proportions and histories of ancient ancestry, followed by many later movements.\nGenes do not carry language, political loyalty, or culture as a package.\nThey can estimate biological relationships among sampled people, but samples remain scarce and uneven.\nThe strongest conclusion is plural formation: the archipelago's people were repeatedly remade through continuity and arrival.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-8a5ec331b6"]}]},{"id":"01.15","number":"01.15","title":"The Communities Called Wa","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.15-p001","text":"Chinese texts bring a few communities of the archipelago into written history.\nThe name Wa appears in accounts written from the perspective of imperial courts and commanderies.\nIn AD 57, a ruler of Na in Wa sent an embassy to the Later Han court and received a seal, according to the dynastic history.\nA gold seal found on Shikanoshima bears an inscription consistent with that grant, though its archaeological context was not scientifically recorded.\nText and object meet here with unusual force and residual uncertainty.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-c46ec45ddf","event-007b5c0ea5","event-49c2a9678d"]},{"id":"01.15-p002","text":"Another embassy arrived in AD 107 with gifts and people described as captives or slaves.\nSuch missions were acts of local as well as international politics.\nA leader who received recognition and valuable objects from a Han emperor could display a relationship unavailable to rivals.\nThe Chinese court, for its part, translated distant communities into its own language of tribute and rank.\nNeither side necessarily understood the transaction in exactly the same way.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-c46ec45ddf","event-007b5c0ea5","event-49c2a9678d"]},{"id":"01.15-p003","text":"The third-century account known as the Gishi Wajinden describes many countries of Wa reached by sea from the Korean commandery of Daifang.\nIts itinerary, distances, offices, customs, and marvels have been debated for centuries.\nIt distinguishes more than thirty polities and gives special attention to a federation centered on Yamatai.\nThis was not yet a description of a unified Japan.\nIt was a report about a fractured but diplomatically connected political world.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-c46ec45ddf","event-007b5c0ea5","event-49c2a9678d"]},{"id":"01.15-p004","text":"The text should be read neither as transparent observation nor as fantasy.\nIts compilers used reports from envoys, conventions of Chinese ethnography, and the strategic concerns of the Wei court.\nSome details can be compared with archaeology, while others resist identification.\nThe famous route to Yamatai cannot be plotted by treating every figure as a modern survey measurement.\nWritten testimony opens a narrow window, shaped by the frame of the observer.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-c46ec45ddf","event-007b5c0ea5","event-49c2a9678d"]}]},{"id":"01.16","number":"01.16","title":"Himiko and the Threshold of Yamato","paragraphs":[{"id":"01.16-p001","text":"According to the Wei history, prolonged warfare among the countries of Wa ended when they agreed to set a woman named Himiko over them.\nShe was said to serve spiritual powers, to remain secluded, and to communicate through a male attendant.\nHer younger brother assisted in government, while guards and women attendants surrounded her residence.\nThe account presents sacred authority and political mediation as inseparable.\nIt does not tell us how her position worked from village to village.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-855fc98b77","event-f3e1071557","event-510a3bdcb6"],"genealogy":[{"id":"AJ1-yamatai-succession","caption":"The links reproduce only the succession and kinship stated by the Wei account.","active":["himiko","himiko-brother","male-successor","iyo"],"row_anchor":{"himiko-brother":"himiko"}}]},{"id":"01.16-p002","text":"Himiko sent envoys to Wei in 238 and received titles, bronze mirrors, cloth, and other gifts.\nThe embassy placed her federation within the diplomacy created by warfare among Wei, Wu, Shu, and the Korean polities.\nRecognition from Wei strengthened Himiko's claims, while her mission extended Wei's symbolic reach across the sea.\nThis was an East Asian political transaction, not an isolated episode in a national chronicle.\nImported prestige and local coalition building had converged.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-855fc98b77","event-f3e1071557","event-510a3bdcb6"],"genealogy":[{"id":"AJ1-yamatai-succession","caption":"The links reproduce only the succession and kinship stated by the Wei account.","active":["himiko","himiko-brother","male-successor","iyo"],"row_anchor":{"himiko-brother":"himiko"}}]},{"id":"01.16-p003","text":"After Himiko died, the text reports a large burial mound, renewed disorder under a male ruler, and restored peace when a young female relative named Iyo or Toyo was installed.\nArchaeology cannot securely attach Himiko's name to a particular tomb.\nThe competing locations proposed for Yamatai, especially northern Kyushu and the Yamato basin, depend on disputed readings of route, chronology, and material power.\nThe uncertainty should remain visible.\nIt is part of the evidence, not a flaw to be concealed.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-855fc98b77","event-f3e1071557","event-510a3bdcb6"],"genealogy":[{"id":"AJ1-yamatai-succession","caption":"The links reproduce only the succession and kinship stated by the Wei account.","active":["himiko","himiko-brother","male-successor","iyo"],"row_anchor":{"himiko-brother":"himiko"}}]},{"id":"01.16-p004","text":"During the third century, very large keyhole-shaped mounds and standardized elite objects began to link powerful groups across central and western Honshu and northern Kyushu.\nThat Kofun transformation belongs to the next chapter, as does the firmer appearance of the Yamato polity.\nHere the threshold is enough.\nAcross tens of thousands of years, communities had navigated, exchanged, settled, farmed, fought, mixed, and negotiated ever wider alliances.\nJapan did not awaken fully formed; a new political order gathered slowly from an ancient maritime world.","mapRefs":["map-1a","map-1b"],"timelineRefs":["event-855fc98b77","event-f3e1071557","event-510a3bdcb6"],"genealogy":[{"id":"AJ1-yamatai-succession","caption":"The links reproduce only the succession and kinship stated by the Wei account.","active":["himiko","himiko-brother","male-successor","iyo"],"row_anchor":{"himiko-brother":"himiko"}}]}]}],"genealogy":{"trees":[{"id":"AJ1-mythic-line","title":"The Retrospective Imperial Genealogy","nodes":[{"id":"amaterasu","name":"Amaterasu","type":"deity","display_date":"mythic"},{"id":"ninigi","name":"Ninigi","type":"deity","display_date":"mythic descendant"},{"id":"jimmu","name":"Jimmu","type":"retrospective first sovereign","display_date":"traditional accession 660 BC"}],"edges":[{"from":"amaterasu","to":"ninigi","relation":"ancestor-of"},{"from":"ninigi","to":"jimmu","relation":"ancestor-of"}]},{"id":"AJ1-yamatai-succession","title":"The Yamatai Succession in the Wei Account","nodes":[{"id":"himiko","name":"Himiko","type":"ruler","display_date":"d. ca. AD 247–248"},{"id":"himiko-brother","name":"Himiko's younger brother","type":"political assistant","display_date":"third century AD"},{"id":"male-successor","name":"Unnamed male successor","type":"disputed ruler","display_date":"third century AD"},{"id":"iyo","name":"Iyo or Toyo","type":"female successor","display_date":"third century AD"}],"edges":[{"from":"himiko","to":"himiko-brother","relation":"sibling"},{"from":"himiko","to":"male-successor","relation":"succeeded-by"},{"from":"male-successor","to":"iyo","relation":"succeeded-by"}]}]},"stats":{"sections":16,"paragraphs":64,"words":4563}};
